{-
   A role written on the wrong kind of declaration, seen by the resolver that compiles `lib/`.

   `Reject.Roles.yana` beside this asks the same question of the ownership stage, which reads
   `prelude/`. Both stages bind roles and both therefore have to answer it, because a `@builtin` the
   reader looks straight past is decoration - and decoration is exactly what `compiler/intrinsic`
   exists to not be. The one that got away is why this fixture exists: `lib/Core/Core.yana` carried
   `@builtin(outcome)` on `Result` rather than on `Outcome` from the day the row existed, and
   nothing said so, because the stage reading that file bound no record roles and found `Outcome` by
   its name instead.

   Each row of `known.def` and `op.def` says which kind of declaration may claim it - a class, a
   record, a signature, a global - and each line below writes one on something else. The message
   names what the row wants rather than what the declaration is, since what the author has to change
   is which declaration carries the attribute.
-}

-- `drop` is a class. A function claiming it would fill nothing and be called by nobody.
@builtin(drop)
fn notAClass(x: I32) -> I32 = x

-- `boolean` is a `data` declaration - two constructors the compiler folds a condition to.
@builtin(boolean)
class NotARecord(a)

-- And the other way: `pointerLoad` is a signature the compiler implements, so a class cannot be it.
@builtin(pointerLoad)
class NotAFunction(a)

-- `commandLineCount` is storage the entry sequence writes, which a function is not.
@builtin(commandLineCount)
fn notAGlobal() -> Size = 0

fn main() -> I32 = notAClass(0)
